const e='<p>Set the display up first with <a href="/guides/connect-iphone-ipad-to-monitor-or-tv">the connection guide</a>.</p>\n\n  <h2>The two kinds of location</h2>\n\n  <p>Open <strong>Files</strong> from the dock. The sidebar has two sections, and the difference matters:</p>\n\n  <figure class="is-narrow is-tall">\n    <img src="/content/images/files-sidebar.webp" alt="The Files sidebar, showing App Locations (App Files, Downloads, Images, Desktop, Trash) and iPhone Locations (iCloud Drive, Field SSD, On My iPhone) with an Add Folder row." width="432" height="1304" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>App Locations live inside XeOS. iPhone Locations are folders elsewhere on your device that you\'ve granted access to.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>App Locations</strong>, App Files, Downloads, Images, Desktop and Trash. These belong to XeOS itself. Downloads is where the browser puts things; Desktop is what you see behind your windows.</li>\n    <li><strong>Your device\'s locations</strong>, folders you\'ve mounted. This is where iCloud Drive, a folder on your phone, and any external drive appear.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h2>Mounting a folder</h2>\n\n  <p>Click <strong>Add Folder</strong> at the bottom of the sidebar (or right-click anywhere in the sidebar and choose Add Folder).</p>\n\n  <figure class="is-narrow is-tall">\n    <img src="/content/images/files-add-folder.webp" alt="The Add Folder row highlighted at the bottom of the Files sidebar." width="432" height="1304" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>One row, three different jobs, local folders, iCloud Drive folders and external drives all mount from here.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>The iOS document picker opens. Navigate to the folder you want and choose it. XeOS works out what kind of folder it is and gives it a matching icon: a cloud for iCloud Drive, a drive glyph for an external disk, a plain folder for local storage. The folder then stays available across restarts, you grant access once.</p>\n\n  <h3>Mounting an iCloud Drive folder</h3>\n\n  <p>In the picker, go to <strong>iCloud Drive</strong> and pick the folder you want, Documents, a project folder, whatever you actually use. It appears in the sidebar with a cloud icon and you can work in it directly: open a file, edit it, save it, and the change syncs back through iCloud the way it would from a Mac.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/files-icloud.webp" alt="The Files app browsing a mounted iCloud Drive folder containing folders and a PDF." width="1600" height="966" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>A mounted iCloud Drive folder, browsed in place.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Files that haven\'t been downloaded from iCloud yet still appear in the listing, and are fetched automatically when you open them, so a folder that\'s mostly still in the cloud looks and behaves like a normal folder.</p>\n\n  <div class="note">\n    <p><strong>Pick a subfolder, not the root.</strong> iOS won\'t let any app enumerate the whole "On My iPhone" store, those are provider roots, not real folders. If you try, XeOS tells you so and asks you to mount a subfolder from that location instead. Mount <em>iCloud Drive › Documents</em> rather than <em>iCloud Drive</em>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Mounting a folder from your phone</h3>\n\n  <p>Same flow: pick a folder under <strong>On My iPhone</strong> (or On My iPad). Anything another app has saved there, a scanner app\'s output, files another app shares, becomes browsable and openable.</p>\n\n  <h3>Mounting an external drive</h3>\n\n  <p>Plug a USB drive or SSD into your adapter\'s USB port. It appears in the iOS document picker under Locations, and mounting it works exactly like mounting anything else.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/files-external-drive.webp" alt="The Files app browsing a mounted external SSD named Field SSD." width="1600" height="966" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>An external SSD, mounted and browsable alongside everything else.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>If the drive is unplugged later, XeOS marks the location unavailable rather than losing it, with a <strong>Check Again</strong> action so you can reconnect and carry on.</p>\n\n  <h3>Hardware worth having</h3>\n\n  <p>A drive needs a port, and video needs a port. On a phone with one connector, that means a hub. What to look for:</p>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>HDMI out</strong>, for the display.</li>\n    <li><strong>USB-A or USB-C data port</strong>, for the drive.</li>\n    <li><strong>Power pass-through</strong>, so the phone charges while it\'s driving a screen and a disk, which it will otherwise not do for long.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>These are usually sold as "USB-C multiport adapters" or "USB-C hubs with power delivery". Bus-powered SSDs work well; large spinning drives may need their own power supply, since a phone supplies limited current.</p>\n\n  <h2>Working with what you\'ve mounted</h2>\n\n  <p>Once a location is in the sidebar it behaves like any other folder:</p>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Open files in place</strong>, double-click a PDF, image, video, text file or Office document and the right viewer opens.</li>\n    <li><strong>Drag and drop</strong> between windows to copy or move, with a progress bar for large transfers.</li>\n    <li><strong>Right-click</strong> for rename, duplicate, compress, unzip, delete and more.</li>\n    <li><strong>Zip and unzip</strong> without any extra tool, double-clicking a <code>.zip</code> extracts it in place.</li>\n    <li><strong>Slideshow</strong> any folder of images (see <a href="/guides/photo-slideshow-on-tv">the slideshow guide</a>).</li>\n    <li><strong>Search</strong> across mounted locations with the system search overlay.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/files-images-folder.webp" alt="A folder of images in the XeOS Files app shown as a grid of thumbnails." width="1600" height="966" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Thumbnails, list view, sorting, a real file manager, not a picker.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <h2>Tidying up the sidebar</h2>\n\n  <p>Right-click any mounted location for its options:</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/files-mount-menu.webp" alt="Right-click menu on a sidebar location: Customize Folder, Pin to Dock, Remove, Add Folder." width="1600" height="966" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Rename and recolour a location, pin it to the dock, or remove it.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Customize Folder</strong>, give it a clearer name, a different icon and a colour. Useful when you have three mounts that all say "Documents".</li>\n    <li><strong>Pin to Dock</strong>, put the folder in the dock so it\'s one click from anywhere.</li>\n    <li><strong>Remove</strong>, take it out of the sidebar. The folder itself is untouched.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>You can also drag locations to reorder them, and drag a folder from the file list onto the sidebar to pin it.</p>\n\n  <div class="cta">\n    <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> turns an iPhone or iPad into a full desktop on any monitor, TV or projector.</p>\n    <p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/external-display-browser/id6758286241" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="app-store-link">Download XeOS on the App Store</a></p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="faq">\n    <h2>Common questions</h2>\n\n    <h3>Do I have to re-grant access every time I open the app?</h3>\n    <p>No. A mounted folder is remembered and stays available across restarts.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I mount the whole of iCloud Drive?</h3>\n    <p>Yes!</p>\n\n    <h3>Does editing a file in a mounted iCloud folder sync back?</h3>\n    <p>Yes. You\'re working on the real file, so saving writes through iCloud like any other app.</p>\n\n    <h3>My external drive disappeared. Is the mount gone?</h3>\n    <p>No, it\'s marked unavailable. Plug the drive back in and use Check Again on the sidebar row.</p>\n\n    <h3>What file systems do external drives need?</h3>\n    <p>Whatever iOS itself can read, exFAT and FAT32 are the safe choices, and APFS and HFS+ drives generally work too. XeOS goes through the system picker, so if the iOS Files app can see the drive, so can XeOS.</p>\n  </div>\n';export{e as default};
//# sourceMappingURL=mount-icloud-local-and-external-drives-DuTduOss.js.map
